<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Dream Trip Around the World</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in team interactions. Communication: Assist in clear communication of plans and roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can resolve misunderstandings by miming or gesturing roles in a group travel project (e.g., uses gestures to show who is in charge of booking transportation and who is responsible for finding accommodations). Can clarify project plans with role cards or diagrams to show responsibilities (e.g., uses labeled visuals to show each group member's role in coordinating a cultural trip). Can repeat and rephrase using gestures to confirm understanding in multilingual settings (e.g., signals agreement by nodding and confirms details with simple phrases).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up (5 min):</b> "Dream Destination Mime." Students think of a unique aspect of their dream trip (e.g., exploring ancient pyramids, hiking a magnificent mountain, experiencing a local tradition) and mime it to their group or the class. Other students guess the activity or destination, using descriptive adjectives like "breathtaking" or "exciting." The focus is on interpreting non-verbal cues and making clear guesses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling (3 min):</b> The teacher models how to clarify a misunderstanding using gestures and simple rephrasing. For example, if a student guesses "swimming" but the mime was "hiking," the teacher would shake their head slightly, then point to their legs and make a climbing motion, saying "No, I was hiking in the mountains. Not swimming." The teacher emphasizes vowel reduction in "could" and "would" when saying, "If I could choose, I would hike the highest peak."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs) (2 min):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to use gestures or rephrase when someone doesn't understand?" (To help them understand, avoid confusion.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When might you need to clarify something during a group project about a global adventure?" (When explaining roles, complex travel plans, or cultural traditions.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material (7 min):</b> The teacher presents a short audio dialogue or written case study (e.g., a "Project Team Meeting Transcript") about a group planning an "Exchange Program" who are struggling to explain their roles and the cultural benefits of their "dream trip" to a potential sponsor. The dialogue includes instances of misunderstanding and moments where clarification is needed, featuring vocabulary like 'perspectives,' 'diversity,' 'benefits,' and 'unforgettable experience'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to recognize communication breakdowns and strategies for clarifying information, particularly regarding project plans and roles, in group settings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main problem the group is facing?" (They are not communicating their project plans and individual roles clearly.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The group clearly understood each other's responsibilities from the start." (False) "The sponsor was impressed by their initial explanation." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match characters in the dialogue with the specific misunderstandings they experienced.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the second conditional for hypothetical travel scenarios, superlatives for comparing destinations, and past continuous to narrate experiences, all within the context of mediating information, clarifying plans, and resolving misunderstandings. They will use vocabulary related to travel, culture, and project management (e.g., 'destination,' 'adventure,' 'understanding,' 'skills,' 'develop,' 'clarify,' 'unique,' 'educational'). Pronunciation practice focuses on 'could' and 'would' reduction and stress in multisyllabic words like 'adVENture' and 'des-ti-NA-tion'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure (15 min):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Clarify the Itinerary" (5 min)</b> The teacher provides sentences about their "Dream Trip" project plans that are intentionally vague or complex (e.g., "We will get to the place by the big river and then find some old things."). Students, in pairs, must rephrase these sentences using clearer language, specific vocabulary (e.g., 'destination', 'explore historical sites'), and possibly the second conditional for alternative plans ("If we couldn't fly, we would travel by train."). They also practice stressing multisyllabic words from the vocabulary list correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled: "Role Card Design & Explain" (5 min)</b> Students work in their "Dream Trip Presentation" groups. Each group designs a simple "Role Card" or diagram for their project, visually representing each member's responsibility (e.g., "The 'Adventurous Explorer': researches unique destinations," "The 'Organized Coordinator': manages budget for the entire trip," "The 'Cultural Ambassador': researches local traditions"). They use adjectives (e.g., 'breathtaking', 'diverse', 'enriching') to describe their roles. Each student then verbally explains their role to another group, ready to rephrase or use gestures if the other group looks confused, demonstrating how they would manage their part if they were exploring the most remote parts of the world.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Misunderstanding Match-Up" (5 min)</b> The teacher provides several "misunderstanding scenarios" related to the "Dream Trip Presentation" (e.g., "Student A thinks Student B is booking accommodation, but Student B thinks they are only finding activities."). In pairs, students role-play these scenarios, with one student expressing confusion and the other mediating to resolve it using rephrasing, gestures, and phrases like, "What I meant was..." or "If you're asking about..." They also incorporate past continuous to narrate how they were planning, e.g., "While I was researching hotels, I was also learning about local traditions."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format (10 min):</b> "Dream Trip Project Clarification Session." In their groups, students conduct a short (2-3 minute) session where they present a key aspect of their "Dream Trip Presentation" project (e.g., the overall itinerary, a specific cultural exchange plan, or the benefits of their chosen destination). During this session, their primary goal is to clearly convey information and proactively use mediation strategies to ensure the audience (classmates) fully understands. They must use their previously created role cards or diagrams to clarify responsibilities and apply the second conditional for hypothetical situations ("If we encountered a problem, we would adapt our plans to explore a different, equally unique tradition"). They will use target vocabulary (e.g., 'adventure,' 'diversity,' 'understanding,' 'perspectives') and practice appropriate pronunciation and stress. Peers are encouraged to ask clarifying questions, and the presenters must respond using rephrasing, gestures, and simple language to ensure comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme & Scenario:</b> This task directly integrates the "Dream Trip Presentation" project with the overarching theme of "A Dream Trip Around the World" and the scenario "Exploring Our Global Community," focusing on the clarity of communication required for such a collaborative, global endeavor. Students practice respectful discussion of cultural differences, a key pragmatic skill, as they explain their travel choices.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, effective use of mediation strategies (rephrasing, gestures, visuals) to ensure communicative task completion and understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy (5 min):</b> Peer Observation Checklist. Students use a simple checklist to assess other groups' clarity and mediation skills during the "Project Clarification Session."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Dream Trip Project Clarification Session" in groups, focusing on clear communication and mediation, while their peers use the checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Examples):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity: Did the group clearly explain their project's key aspects?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of Visuals: Did they effectively use role cards/diagrams to clarify roles/plans?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Rephrasing/Gestures: Did they rephrase ideas or use gestures to resolve misunderstandings?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar & Vocabulary: Was the second conditional used for hypothetical plans? Was target vocabulary (e.g., 'diverse', 'enriching', 'traditions') used effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Were key multisyllabic words (e.g., 'adVENture', 'des-ti-NA-tion') pronounced clearly, and was vowel reduction in 'could'/'would' evident?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration: Did the group members work together to lead the discussion and ensure everyone had input?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one specific mediation strategy (e.g., gesture, rephrasing, visual aid) your group used effectively today to clarify your 'Dream Trip' project?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you could go back and explain one part of your project again, how would you rephrase it to make it even clearer?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt (3 min):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has working on the 'Dream Trip' project and practicing mediation skills enhanced your ability to communicate complex ideas about global cultures and travel experiences?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Thinking about your personal learning, what is the most important 'skill' you gained from exploring our global community through this unit?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the "Project Clarification Session" regarding students' use of mediation strategies and their ability to explain complex plans?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled to clarify their ideas or resolve misunderstandings in future collaborative projects? (e.g., more direct instruction on using specific clarifying phrases, more practice with role-playing potential communication breakdowns).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is Lesson 5, the final lesson in the sequence. It serves as a comprehensive integration of all learned skills, vocabulary, and grammar within the "Exploring Our Global Community" theme, culminating in a practical application of mediation. The next theme will build on these communication and collaborative skills in a new context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>